WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.515
Preserve original thermal files because they may contain radiometric values, metadata, calibration context, palette information, location, and camera settings that exported screenshots lose.

00:00:11.515 --> 00:00:15.040
Keep annotated and processed versions separate.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.385
Use consistent file names tied to mission, target, date, sensor, and sequence.

00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:26.790
Record environmental conditions, distance, angle, operating state, settings, and transformations.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:31.255
Link each report observation to the source file.

00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:37.130
Annotations should identify areas of interest without hiding the surrounding evidence.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:41.595
Include scales and explain palette or range choices.

00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:49.820
Do not use dramatic colors to imply severity that the measurements and context do not support.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.285
Report observations, comparisons, confidence, limitations, and plausible alternatives.

00:00:54.285 --> 00:01:01.100
State whether values are qualitative or radiometric and whether a specialist reviewed them.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.975
Recommend proportionate follow-up rather than announcing a failure from one image.

00:01:06.975 --> 00:01:14.260
Thermal imagery may expose people, occupancy patterns, property conditions, security features, or sensitive operations.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:21.545
Apply access control, encrypted storage and transfer where appropriate, purpose-based retention, and restricted distribution.

00:01:21.545 --> 00:01:30.710
Do not use thermal imaging for stalking, harassment, unlawful surveillance, targeting private individuals, or intrusive observation without authority.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:38.935
Ethical work is necessary, proportionate, transparent to accountable reviewers, and careful about both collection and interpretation.
